{"id":"1b3aa9b0-8326-46bd-9aa3-ea4bf76c9167","article_key":"doi:10.1016/j.cell.2026.09.004","title":"Opposing functions of gut immunomodulatory metabolites on CAR-T therapy","title_zh":"肠道免疫调节代谢物对CAR‑T疗法的相反功能","doi":"10.1016/j.cell.2026.09.004","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2026.09.004","abstract":"Chimeric antigen receptor (CAR)-T cell therapy has transformed hematological cancer treatment, yet nearly half of patients still relapse or progress. Increasing evidence implicates the gut microbiome and antibiotic exposure as key modulators of clinical outcomes. In a cohort of 129 patients across three German centers, shotgun metagenomics and targeted mass spectrometry revealed that reduced short-chain fatty acids, particularly valeric acid, prior to CAR-T cell therapy correlated with increased risk of disease progression. Conversely, high levels of indole metabolites, including indole-3-carboxaldehyde and indole-3-acetic acid as well as the branched-chain fatty acid isovaleric acid, were linked to adverse outcomes. Functional validation in human and murine CAR-T cell models demonstrated that valeric acid supplementation enhanced, while indole-3-carboxaldehyde and isovaleric acid impaired, CAR-T cell efficacy. These findings reveal the opposing roles of immunomodulatory metabolites on CAR-T cell therapy, carrying significant implications for the design of metabolite-guided, microbiome-based therapeutics.","abstract_zh":"嵌合抗原受体（CAR）T细胞疗法已改变血液肿瘤的治疗，但近一半患者仍出现复发或进展。越来越多的证据表明肠道微生物组和抗生素暴露是临床结果的关键调节因子。在德国三家中心的129名患者队列中，采用全基因组测序和靶向质谱发现，CAR‑T细胞治疗前短链脂肪酸（尤其是戊酸）水平降低与疾病进展风险增加相关。相反，吲哚代谢物（包括吲哚-3-羧醛和吲哚-3-乙酸）以及支链脂肪酸异戊酸的高水平与不良结果相关。对人类和小鼠CAR‑T细胞模型的功能验证显示，戊酸补充可增强CAR‑T细胞效能，而吲哚-3-羧醛和异戊酸则抑制CAR‑T细胞功能。这些发现揭示了免疫调节代谢物对CAR‑T疗法的相反作用，对基于代谢物指导的微生物组疗法设计具有重要意义。","snippet":"Chimeric antigen receptor (CAR)-T cell therapy has transformed hematological cancer treatment, yet nearly half of patients still relapse or progress. Increasing evidence implicates the gut microbiome and antibiotic exposure as key modulators of clinical outcomes. In a cohort of 129 patients across three German centers, shotgun metagenomics and targeted mass spectrometry revealed that reduced short-chain fatty acids,…","translated_at":"2026-10-01T04:23:20.028602Z","translation_model":"openai/gpt-oss-20b","source_category":"doi","article_type":"journal-article","volume":"189","issue":"22","published_at":"2026-10-01T00:00:00","acquired_at":"2026-09-30T20:59:22Z","display_date":"2026-09-30T20:59:22Z","display_date_source":"acquired","online_published_at":null,"print_published_at":"2026-10-01T00:00:00","first_author":"Markus Perl","authors_text":"Markus Perl, Severin Guetter, Dhyani Shah, Simon Holzinger, Luisa Becker, Misbah Tariq, Philipp Menauer, Simon Delahais, Gloria Lutzny-Geier, Julian N. Scherer, Sascha Göttert, Konstantin Herfeld, Paul Heinrich, Klaus Georg Kreitmeier, Claudia Gebhard, Wolfgang Herr, Marcel P. Trefny, Janina Doerr, Saba Sameri, Dennis C. Harrer, Kai Ziegler-Martin, Sarah Staudt, Leo Hansmann, Matthias Edinger, Daniel Wolff, Daniela Weber, Elisabeth Meedt, Alexander Denk, Andre Gessner, Ernst Holler, Tobias Wertheimer, Klaus Neuhaus, Tingting Zheng, David M. Cordas Dos Santos, Sebastian Theurich, Melanie Schirmer, Karin Kleigrewe, Jonas Schluter, Marcel van den Brink, Michael Schmitt, Markus Feuerer, Christoph Heuser-Loy, Jeremy Baldwin, Luca Gattinoni, Michael Rehli, Michael Hudecek, Camille Bigenwald, Laurence Zitvogel, Marion Subklewe, Viktoria Blumenberg, Maria-Luisa Schubert, Christoph Stein-Thoeringer, Maik Luu, Sebastian Kobold, Matthias A. Fante, Erik Thiele Orberg, Hendrik Poeck","authors":[{"position":0,"given_name":"Markus","family_name":"Perl","full_name":"Markus Perl","affiliation":null},{"position":1,"given_name":"Severin","family_name":"Guetter","full_name":"Severin Guetter","affiliation":null},{"position":2,"given_name":"Dhyani","family_name":"Shah","full_name":"Dhyani Shah","affiliation":null},{"position":3,"given_name":"Simon","family_name":"Holzinge